> API and interpretation

The API takes its input by value and returns an owned sorted sequence. Separate
input, complete comparison and optional full-state capture policies reject
oversize requests before sorting. Counters exclude copying and allocation.
Histories are produced by the same actual minimum scans as the legacy wrapper.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, volume3: Sorting and Searching, second edition (1998), selection sorting|https://www-cs-faculty.stanford.edu/~knuth/taocp.html]

